#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :question/id #uuid "738bd502-dec6-589b-b198-5040fe7e3906" :question/math-academy-id "e-21806" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y'' + 9\\lambda y = 0, \\qquad y(0) = y(\\pi) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi, \\quad \\lambda \\geq 0$.\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda > 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions.\n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 0\n$$\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n$y'' = 0$.\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions\n\n$$\ny(0) = y(\\pi) = 0\n$$\n\nyields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0$. Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda > 0$.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set\n\n$$\ny = e^{m x}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} + 9λ &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nm = \\pm 3\\textrm i\\sqrt{\\lambda}\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda})\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}) + B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda})\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\cos (0) + B\\sin (0) = 0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A = 0$. So, we have\n\n$$\ny = B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda})\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\sin (3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda}) = 0\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require\n\n$$\n3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda} = n\\pi\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in \\mathbb N$. This gives the set of eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_n = \\dfrac{n^2}{9}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are\n\n$$\ny_n(x) = B_n \\sin(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}), \\qquad B_n \\in\\mathbb R\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/id #uuid "877e1518-96ed-5b31-9e05-22990ce1cb72" :question/math-academy-id "e-21808" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y = 0, \\quad y'(1) = 0, \\quad y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0, \\quad 1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad \\lambda \\in\\mathbb R$.\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda \\neq 0\n$$\n\nseparately, since they correspond to different solutions.\n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 0\n$$\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n$y'' = 0$.\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y'(1) =0$ and\n\n$$\ny(2) + 2y'(2) = 0\n$$\n\nyields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0$. Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider\n\n$$\n\\lambda \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set\n\n$$\ny = e^{m x}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} - 18λm + 81λ^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(m - 9λ)(m - 9λ) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root\n\n$$\nm = 9\\lambda\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{9\\lambda x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = xe^{9\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 9Aλe^{9λx} + Be^{9λx} + 9Bλxe^{9λx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y'(1) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9Aλe^{9λ} + Be^{9λ} + 9Bλe^{9λ} &= 0 \\\\\n9Aλ + B + 9Bλ &= 0 \\\\\n(9λ)A + (1 + 9λ)B &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition\n\n$$\ny(2) + 2y'(2) = 0\n$$\n\nyields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAe^{18λ} + 2Be^{18λ} + 2(9Aλe^{18λ} + Be^{18λ} + 18Bλe^{18λ}) &= 0 \\\\\nA + 2B + 2(9Aλ + B + 18Bλ) &= 0 \\\\\nA + 2B + 18Aλ + 2B + 36Bλ &= 0 \\\\\n(1 + 18λ)A + (4 + 36λ)B &= 0 \\\\\n(1 + 18λ)A + 4(1 + 9λ)B &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{(9λ)A + (1 + 9λ)B = 0; (1 + 18λ)A + 4(1 + 9λ)B = 0\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9λ & 1 + 9λ \\\\ 1 + 18λ & 4(1 + 9λ)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9λ \\cdot 4(1 + 9λ) - (1 + 9λ)(1 + 18λ) &= 0 \\\\\n(1 + 9λ)[36λ - (1 + 18λ)] &= 0 \\\\\n(1 + 9λ)[36λ - 1 - 18λ] &= 0 \\\\\n(1 + 9λ)(18λ - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19 \\textrm{and} \\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider $\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19$. Then, we have the solution\n$y_{1}(x)|= Ae^{9λ_{1}x} + Bxe^{9λ_{1}x}; = Ae^{-x} + Bxe^{-x}$\nand substituting $\\lambda_1$ into\n$(9\\lambda)A + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0$\nwe get\n$-A + \\left(1 -1\\right)B =0$\nwhich gives $A = 0$, and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n$y_1(x) = Bxe^{-x}$.\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18}$, we have\n$y_{2}(x)|= Ae^{9λ_{2}x} + Bxe^{9λ_{2}x}; = Ae^{x/2} + Bxe^{x/2}$\nand substituting $\\lambda_2$ into\n$(9\\lambda)A + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0$\nwe get\n$\\dfrac12A + \\dfrac32 B = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad A = -3B$\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n$y_{2}(x)|=-3Be^{x/2} + Bxe^{x/2}; = Be^{x/2}(x - 3); = Ce^{x/2}(x - 3)$\nwhere we have set $C = B$.\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, respectively, are $Bxe^{-x} \\textrm{and} Ce^{x/2}(x-3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b910453-fc2a-5ed9-ad33-a358bef8bd56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\cos (2λ_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164743} {:answer/id #uuid "f42dfafd-3188-5cca-b8f2-632471a08c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\cos ((λ_{n}x)/(2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164744} {:answer/id #uuid "00f8352c-2e93-5bb2-8d65-ef085d92c19e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\sin (λ_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164745} {:answer/id #uuid "0b4521af-5bc2-508b-9cb6-dbefc6f5d27c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\sin ((λ_{n}x)/(2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164746} {:answer/id #uuid "1330d445-d1e2-58b3-b58d-05aa89e35b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\sin (2λ_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164747} {:answer/id #uuid "1a7b217d-1601-5f20-a990-f727907f7858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B_{n}\\cos (λ_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164748}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d9a5b6d7-8028-531d-bed3-9255d161fbf8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164742} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4607304-a98c-5e83-b6d1-eb02787d636c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164750} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5329c8-d05b-52a0-aafe-ac5a75fb4260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2nπ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164751} {:answer/id #uuid "e36a0b08-b29c-5002-8683-df45359791f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164752} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0b0d9b-555a-50a5-916a-92f9072a48b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(nπ)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164753} {:answer/id #uuid "7408ac64-c03d-588e-b58d-1b73fc52e6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "nπ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164754}] :answer-field/id #uuid "81bd56b0-f643-5799-951e-5039e368bdc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164749}] :question/id #uuid "9fd0718d-5c1d-56bf-aa5a-76fcf93883d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-338667" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y^{″} + 4λ^{2}y = 0, y(0) = y(1) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le 1, λ \\ge 0$.\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem.\nThe eigenvalues are $λ_{n} =$ {{field-1}}, where $n ∈ N$.\n\nThe eigenfunctions are $y_{n}(x) =$ {{field-2}}, where $B_{n} ∈ R$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6986105-a362-5723-a472-8875fcc2fd03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± (1)/(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164778} {:answer/id #uuid "288852bb-f9da-57c3-9992-80630c5febcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (1)/(8)] and [MATH: (1)/(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164779} {:answer/id #uuid "326df2f6-2a07-5975-8a60-6662e2d47494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± (1)/(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164780} {:answer/id #uuid "4323c1bf-b3e7-5a70-909b-423e29b23715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (1)/(4)] and [MATH: (1)/(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164781}] :answer-field/id #uuid "72b57935-3bc0-5c0e-90af-12a2a486a75f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164777} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8dc5077-f04c-57ee-a070-be1ad206c450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Bxe^{− x}] and [MATH: Ce^{2x}(x − 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164783} {:answer/id #uuid "2a544a38-6d62-5995-a3b5-5f8c2c0c954f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Bxe^{− 3x}] and [MATH: Ce^{x}(x − 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164784} {:answer/id #uuid "409ac1df-5681-50c7-9a97-bd6e6d7ec059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Bxe^{2x}] and [MATH: Ce^{− x/2}(x − 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164785} {:answer/id #uuid "5a89c8de-4aac-58be-9ae5-683a387675ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Bxe^{− x}] and [MATH: Ce^{x/2}(x − 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164786}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dd0befae-4840-5c9d-a368-f16d702e8050" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164782}] :question/id #uuid "faaf0705-3b7e-515b-ad1e-e97ff79382c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-338729" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y^{″} - 8λy^{′} + 16λ^{2}y = 0, y^{′}(1) = 0, y(2) + 2y^{′}(2) = 0, 1 \\le x \\le 2, λ ∈ R$.\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\nThe eigenvalues are $λ =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe eigenfunctions are $y =$ {{field-2}}."}]}
